  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The simplest of these sequential adjustments is Holm's method (Holm 1979). The first step is to order the $p$ values for the $n$ hypotheses being tested from smallest to largest, $p(1) \\leq p(2) \\leq \\cdots \\leq p(n)$, and let $H(i)$ be the hypothesis associated with $p(i)$. One proceeds with Holm's method as follows: (i) If $ p(1) > \\pi/n $, accept all $ n $ null hypotheses (i.e., none are declared significant).",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b13",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b14",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b15"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          133.0,
          1113.0,
          279.0,
          1135.0
        ],
        [
          131.0,
          1142.0,
          904.0,
          1233.0
        ],
        [
          169.0,
          1246.0,
          884.0,
          1272.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "1feb04eed3e6d0979e4cb660ba4bef7990626ebf9baa01072ffc24d603102971"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "(ii) If $ p(1) \\leq \\pi/n $, reject $ H(1) $ [i.e., $ H(1) $ is declared significant], and consider $ H(2) $.",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b16"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          171.0,
          1281.0,
          893.0,
          1307.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "05f5fbd25effc12fb2d875d2feb1eca577a1b5e8ae513e2bf5f870a858e2679b"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "(iii) If $ p(2) > \\pi/(n - 1) $, accept $ H(i) $ (for $ i \\geq 2 $).",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b17"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          172.0,
          1316.0,
          588.0,
          1342.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "f34ef2099be6b3f9aec9996f8b7e2b176f8298cd1961cb0119fac3bec91cf3f4"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "(iv) If $ p(2) \\leq \\pi/(n-1) $, reject $ H(2) $ and move onto $ H(3) $.",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b18"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          172.0,
          1351.0,
          671.0,
          1377.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "c86db512845d735f219c42b9c11c4af32c72cdbc6a90986ab770f63ec9cfc719"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "(v) Proceed with rejecting hypotheses until reaching the first i such that $ p(i) > \\pi/(n - i + 1) $.",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b19"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          170.0,
          1387.0,
          981.0,
          1412.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "a0a3265370b85a2e2c1347c8e58dd03f713a05d37ef3a9475fe3f2341e4e87e4"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "We can also apply Holm's method using Equation A4.3a—namely, $ \\alpha = 1 - (1 - \\pi)^{1/n} $, the Dunn-Šidák correction—in place of $ \\alpha = \\pi/n $.",
      "source_page": 1234,
